<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet  xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="elvise">
        <title>Get The Ball, Elvis!</title>
        <introduction>
                <p>
                        Tim Pennings, a mathematics professor, had a dog named Elvis (a Welsh
                        Corgi), and Tim claims that Elvis seemed to know calculus.
                        According to Tim, Elvis could solve optimization problems
                        <mdash/>
                        in particular, he could minimize the time it takes to fetch a ball that Tim throws into Lake Michigan for him.
                </p>
                <p>
                        In this activity, we will model and solve the optimization problem
                        using calculus, and then analyze the problem to see if we can
                        understand exactly how Elvis did it.
                </p>
                <p>
                        We will start with the following assumptions:
                        <ul>
                                <li><p> Elvis' running speed is 6.40<nbsp/>m/s, and </p> </li>
                                <li><p> Elvis' swimming speed is 1.90<nbsp/>m/s.  </p> </li>
                        </ul>
                        
                </p>
                        <figure xml:id="fig_elvis_path">
                                <caption>A possible path for Elvis to follow.</caption>
                                <image width="70%" >
                                        <description><p>A diagram of a possible path that Elvis might take.</p></description>
                                        <xi:include href="./ElvisDiagram.xml" />
                                </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                        The diagram in <xref ref="fig_elvis_path" text="type-local" /> represents the basic set-up of the optimization
                        problem. Elvis starts at the point marked
                        <q><m>E</m></q> The ball lands at the point marked
                        <q><m>B</m></q>
                        The closest point on the
                        shore to where the ball lands is marked
                        <q><m>R</m></q>
                        (because we
                        will use it as a reference point).
                        After the ball is thrown, Elvis
                        runs some distance (possibly zero meters) along the shore toward the
                        reference point, and then swims directly toward the ball. (That is, runs
                        some distance (possibly zero meters) along the shore toward the
                        reference point, and then swims directly toward the ball. (That is,
                        Elvis <em>chooses</em> the point labeled <q><m>S</m></q>.)
                        </p>
                        <p>
                Also, so that we are all using the same references, we will define <m>x</m> to be the distance away from <m>R</m> at which Elvis starts swimming.  That is, <m>x</m> is the distance between <m>R</m> and <m>S</m>.
</p>
<p>
        We are also interested in the angle at which Elvis swims toward the ball, labeled as <m>\theta</m> in the diagram.
</p>

        </introduction>
        <exercise>
                <introduction>
<p>
        Beginning with specific distances, find an optimal path, angle, and time, for Elvis to
        reach the ball. Then, experiment by varying the distances, solve the new problems, and
        generalize your results.
</p>
<p>
        That is, eventually you want a complete generalization: Assuming that the ball lands at so that the nearest point on the shore is <m>L</m><nbsp/>meters away from Elvis and the ball is <m>w</m><nbsp/>meters away from the shore (where both <m>L</m> and <m>w</m> are <em>constants</em>) predict the angle at which Elvis will swim toward the ball and verify your results.
</p>
<p>
        Here is one way you might approach the problem:
</p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                        <p>
                                Assume that the ball lands 7<nbsp/>meters out from a point 10<nbsp/>meters along the shore.
                        </p>
                                
                                         <p> Develop a formula that gives the time for Elvis to reach the ball as a function of the distance <m> x </m> from the reference point at which Elvis begins to swim.  </p> <p> Assign this function to the variable <m> t </m> .  </p> 
                        </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <introduction>
                                <p> Graph <m> y=t(x) </m>. According to your graph, at approximately what distance, <m> x_{*} </m> , from the reference point should Elvis start swimming to minimize the time require to reach the ball? 
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                Use calculus terms to describe the feature(s) of the graph that allow you to determine that the point you chose is in fact the minimum. How much time is required to reach the ball?  Use calculus terms to describe the feature(s) of the graph that allow you to determine that the point you chose is in fact the minimum. In particular,
                </p>
                        </introduction>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                         <p> what can you say about the value of <m> t' </m> at this point?  </p> 
                        </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                         <p> What can you say about the value of <m> t'' </m> at this point?  </p> 
                        </statement>
                </task>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                 <p> Calculate the derivative of your time function, then use your answer to above to set up an equation for Maple to your answer to above to set up an equation for Maple to solve. Verify that your solution agrees with your answer to .  </p> 
                        </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                 <p> Find the time required for Elvis to swim to the ball if he follows the path you described.  </p> 
                        </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                 <p> Find the angle (<m> \theta </m>) at which Elvis will have to swim to follow the path you determined. (Hint: It may be easier to see what is going on if you think about how the <em> cosine </em> of the angle is defined.) </p> 
                        </statement>
                </task>
        </exercise>
