                </exercise>
                <exercise>
                        <statement>
        <p>
                Choose several more values for the starting distance between Tim and
                Elvis. For each, calculate the values of the optimal
                time, the distance
                <m> x </m> (the distance away from Tim at which Elvis will start swimming), and the angle, <m> \theta </m> at which Elvis will swim. Be systematic, and experiment until you find a pattern.
</p>
                        </statement>
                        </exercise>

                        <exercise>
                                <introduction>
        <p>
                Assume that the ball lands 7<nbsp/>meters out from a point <m>L</m><nbsp/>meters along the shore. (Here we are treating <m> L </m> as a <em> parameter </em> . That is, <m> L </m> is constant, but our results may depend on its value.) Based on the pattern you found:
</p>
                                </introduction>
                                <task>
                                        <statement>
                        <p>
                                predict the angle at which Elvis will swim toward the ball;
                        </p>
                                        </statement>
                                </task>
                                <task>
                                        <statement>
                        <p>
                                check your prediction with the values of <m> L </m> that you already used. If necessary, modify your model and prediction so that it will work for every possible value of <m> L </m> . (Your model and prediction may have to be <em>conditional</em> ; that is, you may have to say things like <q> if <m> L </m> is less than <ellipsis/> ,</q> or <q> if the angle between Elvis and the ball is <ellipsis/> .</q>).
        </p>
                                        </statement>
                                </task>
                                </exercise>
                                <exercise>
                                        <introduction>
        <p>
                Assume that the ball lands <m> w </m><nbsp/>meters out from a point <m>L</m><nbsp/>meters along the shore.
</p>
                                        </introduction>
                                        <task>
                                                <statement>
                 <p> Modify your time function to fit the new situation, </p> 
